\pard\plain \s1\qc\b\fs36 FABLES OF AESOP\par
\pard\plain \qc\i\fs24 Four fables in the Rev. George Fyler Townsend\rquote s translation\par
\pard\plain \s1\keepn\b\fs28 THE WOLF AND THE CRANE\par
\pard\plain \fi360\qj\fs24 A WOLF who had a bone stuck in his throat hired a Crane, for a large sum, to put her head into his mouth and draw out the bone.{\cs10\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s2\fs20 {\cs10\super \chftn } Townsend has the Wolf hire the Crane \ldblquote for a large sum\rdblquote .}} When the Crane had extracted the bone and demanded the promised payment, the Wolf, grinning and grinding his teeth, exclaimed: \ldblquote Why, you have surely already had a sufficient recompense, in having been permitted to draw out your head in safety from the mouth and jaws of a wolf.\rdblquote \par
\pard\plain \li720\i\fs24 In serving the wicked, expect no reward, and be thankful if you escape injury for your pains.\par
\page
\pard\plain \s1\keepn\b\fs28 THE FATHER AND HIS SONS\par
\pard\plain \fi360\qj\fs24 A FATHER had a family of sons who were perpetually quarreling among themselves. When he failed to heal their disputes by his exhortations, he determined to give them a practical illustration of the evils of disunion; and for this purpose he one day told them to bring him a bundle of sticks. When they had done so, he placed the faggot into the hands of each of them in succession, and ordered them to break it in pieces. They tried with all their strength, and were not able to do it. He next opened the faggot, took the sticks separately, one by one, and again put them into his sons\rquote  hands, upon which they broke them easily. He then addressed them in these words: \ldblquote My sons, if you are of one mind, and unite to assist each other, you will be as this faggot, uninjured by all the attempts of your enemies; but if you are divided among yourselves, you will be broken as easily as these sticks.\rdblquote \par
\pard\plain \qc{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw26\pich26\picwgoal15\pichgoal15 89504e470d0a1a0a0000000d49484452000000010000000108060000001f15c4890000000d49444154789c63f8ffff3f0005fe02fea7d7a5a10000000049454e44ae426082}}{\nonshppict{\pict\wmetafile8 0100}}\par
\page
\pard\plain \s1\keepn\b\fs28 THE BAT AND THE WEASELS\par
\pard\plain \fi360\qj\fs24 A BAT who fell upon the ground and was caught by a Weasel pleaded to be spared his life. The Weasel refused, saying that he was by nature the enemy of all birds. The Bat assured him that he was not a bird, but a mouse, and thus was set free. Shortly afterwards the Bat again fell to the ground and was caught by another Weasel, whom he likewise entreated not to eat him. The Weasel said that he had a special hostility to mice. The Bat assured him that he was not a mouse, but a bat, and thus a second time escaped.\par
\pard\plain \li720\i\fs24 It is wise to turn circumstances to good account.\par
\page
\pard\plain \s1\keepn\b\fs28 THE SWALLOW AND THE CROW\par
\pard\plain \fi360\qj\fs24 THE SWALLOW and the Crow had a contention about their plumage. The Crow put an end to the dispute by saying, \ldblquote Your feathers are all very well in the spring, but mine protect me against the winter.\rdblquote \par
\pard\plain \li720\i\fs24 Fair weather friends are not worth much.\par
\pard\plain \fs24 The same lesson is a Russian proverb: {\f1 \'d2\'e8\'f8\'e5 \'e5\'e4\'e5\'f8\'fc \emdash  \'e4\'e0\'eb\'fc\'f8\'e5 \'e1\'f3\'e4\'e5\'f8\'fc.}\par
